{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>There He made for him a statute and an ordinance, and there He tested him</b> — At Marah He gave them some of the sections of the Torah for them to engage in: the Sabbath, the red heifer, and civil laws. <b>And there He tested him</b> — to the people; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 15:25), and it is the view of our Sages (Sanhedrin 56b). And I wonder: why did He not explain these statutes and ordinances here and say, \"And YHWH spoke to Moses, commanding the children of Israel,\" as He said concerning the sections mentioned above, \"Speak to the entire congregation of the children of Israel\" (Exodus 12:3), and so He would do with all the commandments in the Tent of Meeting, in the plains of Moab, and the Passover of the desert (Numbers 9:2)? And according to Rashi's language, that He said \"sections for them to engage in,\" it implies that He made known to them those statutes and taught them — that HaKadosh Barukh Hu is destined to command you in these matters, in the manner that He taught our father Abraham the Torah. And this would have been to accustom them to the commandments and to know whether they would accept them with joy and gladness of heart. And that is the trial of which it says <b>and there He tested him</b>. And He informed them that He would yet command them in commandments — this is what it says: <b>If you will diligently heed the voice of YHWH your Elohim and do what is right in His eyes, and give ear to His commandments</b> — which He would command them.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense: when they began to come into the great and awesome wilderness, a place of thirst where there was no water, He established for them, for their sustenance and their needs, regulations (חֻקִּים) that they would follow until they came to an inhabited land. For a regular practice is called חֹק, as in \"feed me my allotted bread\" (חֻקִּי) (Proverbs 30:8), \"the statutes (חֻקּוֹת) of heaven and earth\" (Jeremiah 33:25). And it is called מִשְׁפָּט when it is properly measured, as in \"thus David did and thus was his judgment (מִשְׁפָּט) all the days\" (1 Samuel 27:11), \"according to the former judgment (מִשְׁפָּט) with which you provided his drink\" (Genesis 40:13), \"and a palace shall sit upon its appointed place (מִשְׁפָּט)\" (Jeremiah 30:18). The meaning is either according to its measure, or that He would discipline them with the statutes of the wilderness — to endure hunger and thirst, to call to Him through them, not by way of complaint. And the ordinances (מִשְׁפָּטִים) were how they would live by them: that each man should love his fellow, and conduct themselves according to the counsel of the elders, and walk modestly in their tents in matters of women and children, and that they should conduct themselves peacefully with those who came into the camp to sell them something, and ethical reproofs, so that they would not be like the camps of plunderers who do every abomination and feel no shame — as it says in the Torah, \"When you go out as a camp against your enemies, keep yourself from every evil thing\" (Deuteronomy 23:10).\n\nAnd so in Joshua it says, \"And Joshua made a covenant for the people on that day, and set for them a statute and an ordinance in Shechem\" (Joshua 24:25) — these are not statutes of the Torah and its ordinances, but rather regulations and the settlement of the polity, such as the conditions that Joshua stipulated, which the Sages mentioned (Bava Kamma 80b), and similar things. And it says <b>and there He tested him</b> — to make known that leading them on that road where there was no water and bringing them to the bitter waters was for a trial, as it says, \"He afflicted you and let you hunger... in order to test you, to do you good in your end\" (Deuteronomy 8:16)." "And the meaning of <b>And YHWH showed him a tree</b> is that He showed him a tree and said to him: \"Cast this tree into the waters and they will be sweetened.\" And since I have not found the root of מוֹרֶה except in the sense of teaching — \"He taught me (וַיֹּרֵנִי) and said to me\" (Proverbs 4:4), meaning He taught me, and so all of them — it appears according to the plain sense that that tree sweetens the waters by its nature, that this is an innate property in it, and He taught it to Moses. And our Sages said (Mekhilta and Tanḥuma here) that the tree was bitter, and it was a miracle within a miracle, like the salt that Elisha cast into the waters (2 Kings 2:21). And if so, He said <b>וַיּוֹרֵהוּ</b> because the tree was not found in that place, and HaKadosh Barukh Hu showed him its location, or produced it for him by miracle. And furthermore, I found in the Yelamdenu (Tanḥuma here): See what is written there — <b>And YHWH showed him (וַיּוֹרֵהוּ) a tree</b>; it does not say וַיַּרְאֵהוּ, but rather <b>וַיּוֹרֵהוּ</b> — He showed him His way, that is to say, He showed him and taught him the way of HaKadosh Barukh Hu, that He sweetens the bitter with the bitter."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The abbreviation הקב\"ה is rendered as HaKadosh Barukh Hu throughout, per the standard convention for reverent divine epithets. The Aramaic loanword ילמדנו in the second comment refers to the opening formula of the Tanḥuma midrash."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:09:17.879403797Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}